INT. TOLL BOOTH - HIGHWAY 9 - 3 A.M.

A glass box at the end of nowhere. Sodium light. A fan turns in one corner. Rain on the roof.

LEILANI KANE, 50, in a reflective vest, sits with a voice recorder pressed to her mouth and a thermos steaming at her elbow.

LEILANI
(to recorder)
Day eleven. I said I'd do this every night. I'm doing it.

She sips. Sets the thermos down.

LEILANI (CONT'D)
(to recorder)
Nobody has come through since two-forty. A truck, then nothing. I counted the trucks on the first night, you know. Eighty-one. Last night, nine. They've found another road. That's what I would do. I'd find another road.

She clicks the recorder off. Clicks it on.

LEILANI (CONT'D)
(to recorder)
Play back.

She presses a button. A scratchy sound.

LEILANI (V.O.)
(recorded)
This is Leilani Kane. If you are hearing this, either I am dead or I got brave. Probably I am neither. Probably I am in the booth with a thermos telling the dark what it already knows.

She laughs, and it hurts. She stops the playback.

LEILANI
(to recorder)
That's from March. March, Leilani. You were so sure you'd be somewhere else.

A pause. Rain.

LEILANI (CONT'D)
(to recorder)
I'm going to say a thing now. I haven't said it to anyone. When the state sold the road to the company, I thought, fine, fine, I'll stay on. They'll need somebody. And there was a letter, and the letter said my position had been eliminated, effective the first, and I read it, and I folded it, and I came to work. Seven weeks. Nobody noticed. Nobody has paid me since August.

She looks at the barrier arm, a red-and-white bar pointing at the sky.

LEILANI (CONT'D)
(to recorder)
I keep the arm up. Isn't that something? There's nothing to stop. There's no one to stop. I keep the arm up so they'll know the road is still open.

She switches off the fan. In the sudden quiet she holds the recorder in both hands.

LEILANI (CONT'D)
(to recorder)
If you're hearing this, I'm going home now. I'm going to leave the lights on. If a car comes through, it can have the road.

She stands. Hangs up her vest. Leaves the recorder on the counter, red light glowing.
